-- Q1: medium / L3 — ISO AMT credit carryforward
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Greenleaf Corp employee Mia Torrance exercised ISOs in 2025 and paid $18,000 in AMT attributable solely to the ISO bargain element. In 2026, her regular tax liability exceeds her tentative minimum tax by $12,000. How much of her prior-year AMT credit may she use in 2026?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$12,000", "$18,000", "$6,000", "$0"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 53, AMT paid due to deferral items (such as ISO exercises) generates a minimum tax credit that can offset regular tax in future years. The credit is limited to the excess of the regular tax over the tentative minimum tax in the carryforward year. Since Mia's regular tax exceeds her TMT by $12,000, she may use $12,000 of her $18,000 credit, carrying the remaining $6,000 forward. Wrong (B): The full $18,000 credit cannot be used because it is limited to the regular-tax-over-TMT excess. Wrong (C): $6,000 is the remaining carryforward amount, not the usable credit. Wrong (D): The credit is not forfeited; deferral-item AMT generates a refundable-style carryforward credit under Section 53.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Section 409A specified employee 6-month delay
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Clearwater Industries (a publicly traded company) maintains a Section 409A nonqualified deferred compensation plan. Chief Operating Officer Daniel Frost, a specified employee, separates from service on April 1, 2026, with $400,000 of deferred compensation payable upon separation. The plan provides for lump-sum distribution upon separation from service. Frost's tax advisor must determine the earliest permissible payment date. When may Clearwater distribute the deferred compensation to Frost?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["April 1, 2026, the date of separation", "October 1, 2026, six months after separation", "January 1, 2027, the first day of the following calendar year", "April 15, 2027, six months after the end of the separation quarter"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 409A(a)(2)(B)(i), distributions to a specified employee of a publicly traded company upon separation from service must be delayed at least six months from the date of separation. Frost separated on April 1, 2026, so the earliest permissible payment date is October 1, 2026. This anti-abuse rule prevents executives from timing departures to accelerate payments. Wrong (A): Immediate payment violates the six-month delay rule for specified employees. Wrong (C): The delay is measured from the separation date, not tied to a calendar-year boundary. Wrong (D): The six-month period runs from the separation date, not from the end of a fiscal quarter.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Restricted stock dividends before vesting (no 83(b))
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Oakridge Manufacturing grants employee Carlos Reyes 5,000 shares of restricted stock on March 1, 2026. The shares vest in three years. Reyes does not file a Section 83(b) election. During the restriction period, Oakridge pays a $2.00 per share annual dividend on the restricted shares. How are the dividend payments treated for tax purposes during the vesting period?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As qualified dividend income eligible for preferential rates", "As a nontaxable return of capital", "As ordinary compensation income", "As tax-deferred income recognized at vesting"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 83, when an employee holds restricted stock without filing an 83(b) election, the employee is not yet treated as the owner for tax purposes. Dividends paid on unvested restricted stock are treated as additional compensation (ordinary income), not as qualified dividends. The employer may deduct these payments as compensation expense. Wrong (A): Qualified dividend treatment requires the recipient to be the beneficial owner; without an 83(b) election, the recipient is not yet the owner for tax purposes. Wrong (B): The dividends are not a return of capital because they represent compensation for services. Wrong (D): Dividend payments on unvested restricted stock are taxable when received, not deferred until vesting.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — NQSO grant with readily ascertainable FMV
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Pinnacle Systems grants CFO Yuki Tanaka 10,000 nonqualified stock options on January 2, 2026. The options are actively traded on an established securities market and have a readily ascertainable fair market value of $8 per option at the grant date. The exercise price is $45 per share, and the current stock FMV is $50. What are the tax consequences to Tanaka at the grant date?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No tax consequence because NQSOs are never taxed at grant", "Capital gain of $50,000 on the spread between FMV and exercise price", "Ordinary income of $50,000 on the stock spread at grant", "Ordinary income of $80,000 on the fair market value of the options"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.83-7, when a nonqualified stock option has a readily ascertainable FMV at grant (e.g., it is actively traded on an established market), the employee recognizes ordinary income equal to the FMV of the option at grant. Tanaka recognizes 10,000 x $8 = $80,000 of ordinary income. The option FMV, not the stock spread, is the measure. Wrong (A): NQSOs with a readily ascertainable FMV are taxed at grant under Section 83. Wrong (B): The spread between stock FMV and exercise price is not the correct measure; the option's own FMV controls. Wrong (C): $50,000 incorrectly uses the stock spread rather than the option's traded FMV.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Section 409A short-term deferral exception
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Brightstone Consulting promises VP of Sales Nadia Petrova a $100,000 performance bonus on December 15, 2026, contingent on meeting annual revenue targets. The bonus vests on December 31, 2026 (no longer subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture). Brightstone pays the bonus on February 28, 2027. A tax advisor evaluates whether this arrangement is subject to Section 409A. What is the correct analysis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The arrangement is exempt under the short-term deferral rule because payment occurs within 2.5 months after the end of the taxable year in which the bonus vests", "The arrangement violates Section 409A because payment was not made in the same year as vesting", "The arrangement is exempt because all performance bonuses are excluded from Section 409A", "The arrangement must comply with Section 409A because the bonus exceeds the annual deferral limit"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.409A-1(b)(4), the short-term deferral exception provides that compensation is not subject to Section 409A if it is paid by the 15th day of the third month (2.5 months) following the end of the taxable year in which the amount is no longer subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture. The bonus vested December 31, 2026, and payment by March 15, 2027 satisfies the exception. Wrong (B): Section 409A does not require same-year payment; the short-term deferral rule allows a 2.5-month window. Wrong (C): Performance bonuses are not categorically excluded from Section 409A; only the short-term deferral exception applies here. Wrong (D): Section 409A has no annual deferral dollar limit; it is a timing and distribution rule.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Employer deduction timing for restricted stock
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Ironclad Technologies grants employee Liam Chen 3,000 shares of restricted stock on June 1, 2026, when FMV is $20 per share. Chen does not make a Section 83(b) election. The shares vest on June 1, 2029, when FMV is $55 per share. When does Ironclad receive its compensation deduction, and for what amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["June 1, 2026, for $60,000", "June 1, 2029, for $165,000", "June 1, 2029, for $60,000", "No deduction is allowed for restricted stock grants"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 83(h), the employer's deduction for restricted stock is allowed in the taxable year in which the employee includes the amount in income. Without an 83(b) election, the employee recognizes income at vesting. Ironclad deducts $165,000 (3,000 shares x $55 FMV at vesting) in 2029, matching Chen's ordinary income inclusion. Wrong (A): Without an 83(b) election, no deduction is allowed at grant because the employee has not yet recognized income. Wrong (C): $60,000 uses the grant-date FMV, which is only correct if an 83(b) election was filed. Wrong (D): Employers are entitled to a deduction for restricted stock compensation equal to the employee's income inclusion.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Performance-based vesting and Section 83
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Nexus Biotech grants employee Dr. Hana Osei 8,000 shares of restricted stock on January 1, 2026, when FMV is $15 per share. The shares vest only if the company achieves FDA approval of its lead drug candidate — a condition that may never occur. Dr. Osei does not file a Section 83(b) election. On September 30, 2028, FDA approval is obtained and the shares vest when FMV is $62 per share. What is the correct tax treatment for Dr. Osei?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$120,000 ordinary income on January 1, 2026", "No taxable event because performance-based vesting is exempt from Section 83", "$496,000 ordinary income on September 30, 2028", "$376,000 capital gain on September 30, 2028"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 83(a), property transferred in connection with services is included in income when it is no longer subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture. A performance condition (FDA approval) that may never be satisfied constitutes a substantial risk of forfeiture. Without an 83(b) election, Dr. Osei recognizes 8,000 x $62 = $496,000 of ordinary income when the condition is met and the shares vest. Wrong (A): No income is recognized at grant because the shares are subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture. Wrong (B): Performance-based restricted stock is not exempt from Section 83; it follows the standard rules. Wrong (D): The gain at vesting is ordinary income, not capital gain, because it represents compensation for services.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Net unrealized appreciation (NUA) in employer securities
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Rita Espinoza, age 62, takes a lump-sum distribution of $200,000 in employer stock from her qualified 401(k) plan. Her cost basis in the stock within the plan is $50,000, and the stock's current FMV is $200,000. She elects NUA treatment and immediately transfers the stock to a taxable brokerage account. What is the tax treatment of the $150,000 of net unrealized appreciation at the time of the lump-sum distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Taxed as ordinary income in the year of distribution", "Taxed as short-term capital gain in the year of distribution", "Excluded from income entirely and never taxed", "Not taxed at distribution; taxed as long-term capital gain when the stock is sold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 402(e)(4), net unrealized appreciation in employer securities distributed from a qualified plan is not included in income at the time of distribution. The NUA is taxed as long-term capital gain when the stock is ultimately sold, regardless of the subsequent holding period. Only the cost basis ($50,000) is taxed as ordinary income at distribution. Wrong (A): Only the cost basis is ordinary income at distribution; the NUA is deferred. Wrong (B): NUA is not taxed at distribution at all; it receives LTCG treatment at sale. Wrong (C): The NUA is eventually taxed as LTCG upon sale; it is not permanently excluded.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — ISO exercise price must equal or exceed FMV
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Redstone Analytics proposes granting CEO Julia Park incentive stock options with an exercise price of $35 per share when the stock's FMV is $42 per share. The company's tax advisor reviews the proposed terms. What issue does the advisor identify regarding the exercise price?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The options fail to qualify as ISOs because the exercise price is below FMV at the grant date", "The options qualify as ISOs but the $7 discount is taxable at grant", "The options qualify as ISOs because the exercise price is within 20% of FMV", "The options are treated as NQSOs only if the employee exercises within one year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 422(b)(4), an incentive stock option must have an exercise price equal to or greater than the fair market value of the stock at the time of grant. An exercise price of $35 when FMV is $42 fails this requirement, and the options cannot qualify as ISOs. Wrong (B): The options do not qualify as ISOs at all; there is no partial qualification with a discount. Wrong (C): There is no 20% tolerance; the exercise price must meet or exceed 100% of FMV (110% for 10% shareholders). Wrong (D): The holding period is irrelevant to the exercise price requirement; the options simply do not qualify as ISOs.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: medium / L3 — ISO AMT basis adjustment at qualifying sale
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Westfield Engineering employee Thomas Ng exercised ISOs on 2,000 shares at an exercise price of $25 when FMV was $60 per share. He reported a $70,000 AMT preference item in the exercise year. Two years after grant and one year after exercise, he sells all shares for $85 per share. For regular tax purposes, his gain is $120,000 ($85 - $25 = $60 x 2,000). What is his gain for AMT purposes on the qualifying sale?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$120,000", "$50,000", "$70,000", "$0"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): For AMT purposes, the basis in ISO shares includes the bargain element recognized as an AMT preference item at exercise. Thomas's AMT basis is $60 per share (exercise price $25 + $35 bargain element). His AMT gain on sale is ($85 - $60) x 2,000 = $50,000, compared to $120,000 for regular tax. This difference generates a minimum tax credit. Wrong (A): $120,000 is the regular tax gain using the $25 exercise price basis, not the AMT basis. Wrong (C): $70,000 is the AMT preference item from the exercise year, not the gain at sale. Wrong (D): There is a gain for AMT purposes because the sale price ($85) exceeds the AMT basis ($60).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Section 409A subsequent deferral election
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Atlas Financial maintains a Section 409A NQDC plan for senior executives. VP of Operations Grant Ellis originally elected to receive his $500,000 deferred balance in a lump sum upon separation from service. Ellis now wishes to change the distribution to five annual installments beginning at separation. His tax advisor reviews the requirements for a subsequent deferral election under Section 409A. Which conditions must the new election satisfy?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The election must be submitted 30 days before the original payment date", "The election requires employer consent but has no timing restrictions", "The election must be made at least 12 months before the original payment date and must delay the first payment by at least 5 years", "The election must be made before the beginning of the calendar year in which separation occurs"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 409A(a)(4)(C), a subsequent deferral election must meet three requirements: (1) the election cannot take effect for at least 12 months after it is made, (2) the first payment under the new schedule must be delayed at least 5 years from the date the payment would have been made, and (3) for time-based payments, the election must be made at least 12 months before the originally scheduled date. Wrong (A): A 30-day advance period is insufficient; the statute requires at least 12 months. Wrong (B): The election has strict timing requirements beyond employer consent. Wrong (D): The prior-year timing rule applies to initial deferral elections, not subsequent deferral elections.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Multi-component compensation evaluation
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Apex Dynamics offers newly hired CTO Dr. Kenji Watanabe a compensation package: (1) salary of $450,000, (2) 20,000 ISOs with exercise price at FMV of $30, (3) 10,000 shares of restricted stock (FMV $30, 4-year vest), and (4) $200,000 annual NQDC deferral. Dr. Watanabe's advisor evaluates the tax efficiency of each component. Assuming Dr. Watanabe expects the stock to appreciate significantly, is in the 37% bracket throughout, and the company is financially stable, which element of the package provides the greatest long-term tax advantage?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The NQDC deferral, because Section 409A eliminates tax on deferred amounts permanently", "The salary, because W-2 wages receive the most favorable tax treatment", "The restricted stock without an 83(b) election, because appreciation is taxed as capital gain at vesting", "The ISOs with an 83(b)-style early exercise, because qualifying disposition treatment converts all appreciation to long-term capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): ISOs provide the greatest long-term tax advantage when stock appreciation is expected. Under Sections 421-424, a qualifying disposition (holding 2 years from grant, 1 year from exercise) treats the entire gain as long-term capital gain at preferential rates (20% vs. 37% ordinary). Early exercise minimizes the AMT preference item and starts the holding period clock. No other component converts compensation entirely to LTCG. Wrong (A): Section 409A only defers taxation; deferred amounts are taxed as ordinary income when distributed. Wrong (B): Salary is taxed at the highest ordinary rates with no deferral or capital gain potential. Wrong (C): Without an 83(b) election, restricted stock appreciation at vesting is ordinary income, not capital gain.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Section 132 qualified employee discount for goods
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Hartfield Retail allows employees to purchase merchandise at a 35% discount. The company's gross profit percentage on merchandise is 40%. Employee Danielle Brooks purchases a $500 item for $325 (a 35% discount). How much of the discount must Brooks include in gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$0, because the entire discount is excludable", "$25, the excess over the gross profit percentage", "$75, the full discount amount", "$175, the discount below the employer's cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 132(c), a qualified employee discount on goods is excludable up to the employer's gross profit percentage. Hartfield's gross profit percentage is 40%, so employees may receive up to a 40% discount tax-free. Since Brooks's 35% discount is less than the 40% gross profit percentage, the entire discount is excludable. Wrong (B): $25 would be correct if the gross profit percentage were 30%, but it is 40%, making the full 35% discount excludable. Wrong (C): The discount is not fully taxable; Section 132(c) provides an exclusion up to the gross profit percentage. Wrong (D): $175 confuses the discount with the employer's cost; the exclusion is based on the gross profit percentage, not the cost.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — ISO 10-year exercise period
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$What is the maximum period after the grant date within which an incentive stock option must be exercised to retain ISO qualification?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["5 years", "10 years", "15 years", "20 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 422(b)(3), an incentive stock option must by its terms not be exercisable after the expiration of 10 years from the date the option is granted. This is a statutory requirement for ISO qualification. Wrong (A): Five years is the exercise period for ISOs granted to 10% shareholders, not the general rule. Wrong (C): There is no 15-year ISO exercise provision in the Internal Revenue Code. Wrong (D): Twenty years exceeds the statutory maximum; ISOs must expire within 10 years.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Section 83(b) election 30-day deadline
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Within what time period must a Section 83(b) election be filed after the transfer of restricted property?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["60 days", "90 days", "30 days", "By the due date of the tax return for the year of transfer"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 83(b)(2), the election must be filed with the IRS within 30 days after the date of the property transfer. This deadline is absolute — there are no extensions and no exceptions. Filing late renders the election void. Wrong (A): Sixty days exceeds the statutory filing window. Wrong (B): Ninety days is not the correct deadline; the statute specifies 30 days. Wrong (D): The tax return due date is too late; the election must be filed within 30 days of transfer, well before the return is due.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — 10% shareholder special ISO rules
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Silverline Corp (FMV $50 per share) plans to grant ISOs to two employees. Employee A owns 3% of Silverline's voting stock. Employee B owns 12% of Silverline's voting stock. The proposed terms for both grants are: exercise price of $50 per share, exercisable over 10 years. A tax advisor reviews the grants for compliance with ISO requirements. The advisor determines that one grant must be restructured. Which grant requires modification, and what changes are needed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Employee A's grant must use an exercise price of $55 and a 5-year term", "Employee B's grant must use an exercise price of $50 and a 7-year term", "Both grants must use an exercise price of $55 and a 5-year term", "Employee B's grant must use an exercise price of at least $55 and a term of no more than 5 years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 422(c)(5), when an ISO is granted to an employee who owns more than 10% of the total combined voting power, two special rules apply: (1) the exercise price must be at least 110% of FMV at grant (110% x $50 = $55), and (2) the option must not be exercisable more than 5 years from grant. Employee B (12% owner) triggers both requirements. Employee A (3%) uses standard rules and needs no modification. Wrong (A): Employee A is below the 10% threshold and does not require modified terms. Wrong (B): The exercise price must be at least 110% of FMV ($55), not $50, and the term must be 5 years, not 7. Wrong (C): Only Employee B exceeds the 10% ownership threshold; Employee A's standard terms are compliant.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Employer gets no deduction on ISO qualifying disposition
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$What is the employer's tax deduction when an employee makes a qualifying disposition of shares acquired through incentive stock options?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No deduction is allowed", "A deduction equal to the employee's long-term capital gain", "A deduction equal to the bargain element at exercise", "A deduction equal to the fair market value of the shares at sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 421(a), when an employee makes a qualifying disposition of ISO shares (meeting the 2-year-from-grant and 1-year-from-exercise holding periods), the employer receives no compensation deduction. This is a key tax disadvantage of ISOs from the employer's perspective. Wrong (B): The employer cannot deduct the employee's capital gain; no deduction exists for qualifying dispositions. Wrong (C): The bargain element deduction is available only for disqualifying dispositions, not qualifying ones. Wrong (D): Fair market value at sale has no bearing on the employer's deduction because no deduction is permitted.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Cash vs accrual timing for bonus
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Westbrook Consulting (accrual method) accrues a $150,000 year-end bonus for marketing director Claire Dunlap (cash method, unrelated party) on December 31, 2026. The bonus is paid on January 25, 2027. In which taxable year may Westbrook deduct the bonus, and in which year must Dunlap include it in income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Westbrook deducts in 2027; Dunlap includes in 2026", "Westbrook deducts in 2026; Dunlap includes in 2027", "Both deduct and include in 2026", "Both deduct and include in 2027"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 461(h) and the recurring item exception, an accrual-method employer may deduct a bonus accrued by year-end if it is paid within 2.5 months of the close of the taxable year (by March 15, 2027). January 25 satisfies this. Claire, a cash-method taxpayer, includes the bonus in income when actually received in 2027. This timing mismatch is permitted because the parties are unrelated. Wrong (A): Westbrook deducts in 2026 under the accrual method, not 2027. Wrong (C): Dunlap is a cash-method taxpayer and does not include the bonus until received in 2027. Wrong (D): Westbrook's deduction falls in 2026 under accrual-method rules, not 2027.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Section 409A anti-acceleration rule
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 409A, what is the general rule regarding the acceleration of payments from a nonqualified deferred compensation plan?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Acceleration is permitted with 30 days' notice to the IRS", "Acceleration is permitted if the employee consents in writing", "Acceleration of payments is generally prohibited", "Acceleration is permitted only for amounts under $50,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 409A(a)(3), a NQDC plan may not permit the acceleration of the time or schedule of any payment. This anti-acceleration rule is a fundamental requirement of Section 409A, with only limited regulatory exceptions (such as plan termination or domestic relations orders). Wrong (A): Notice to the IRS does not authorize acceleration; the prohibition is statutory. Wrong (B): Employee consent does not override the anti-acceleration rule. Wrong (D): There is no dollar threshold below which acceleration is permitted under Section 409A.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — Split-dollar life insurance equity vs loan regime
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Crestline Holdings establishes a split-dollar life insurance arrangement for its CEO, Patricia Voss. Under the arrangement, Crestline pays $40,000 in annual premiums on a $2,000,000 policy. Voss's family is the beneficiary of the death benefit in excess of the employer's premium recovery. The arrangement names Crestline as the policy owner. A tax advisor must determine the proper tax treatment. Under current regulations, how should the arrangement be characterized, and what are the tax consequences to Voss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Equity regime — Voss is taxed on the full premium of $40,000 as compensation", "Loan regime — Voss is taxed on imputed interest on the cumulative premiums", "Equity regime — Voss is taxed on the economic benefit (Table 2001 cost of current insurance protection)", "Economic benefit regime — Voss is taxed on the Table 2001 cost of current life insurance protection provided, because the employer is the policy owner"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. Sections 1.61-22 and 1.7872-15, when the employer owns the policy (endorsement method), the economic benefit regime applies. Voss is taxed annually on the value of current life insurance protection (measured by Table 2001 rates) minus any amount she pays toward the premium. The full premium is not income because the employer retains a recovery interest. Wrong (A): The equity regime applies when the employee owns the policy, not when the employer does. Wrong (B): The loan regime applies to collateral assignment arrangements where the employee owns the policy, not to employer-owned policies. Wrong (C): While the economic benefit measure is correct, the characterization as "equity regime" is wrong; this is the economic benefit regime because the employer is the owner.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L3 — ISO disqualifying disposition creates employer deduction
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Vanguard Electronics grants employee Marco Silva ISOs to purchase 3,000 shares at $28 per share. Silva exercises when FMV is $52 per share and sells the shares eight months later for $60 per share. This is a disqualifying disposition. What deduction does Vanguard receive, and when?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Vanguard deducts $72,000 in the year of the disqualifying disposition", "Vanguard deducts $96,000 in the year of the disqualifying disposition", "Vanguard deducts $24,000 in the year of the disqualifying disposition", "Vanguard receives no deduction because ISOs never generate an employer deduction"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 421(b), when an employee makes a disqualifying disposition of ISO shares, the employer receives a compensation deduction equal to the ordinary income recognized by the employee. Silva's ordinary income is the lesser of the bargain element at exercise ($52 - $28 = $24 x 3,000 = $72,000) or the gain on the sale ($60 - $28 = $32 x 3,000 = $96,000). The ordinary income is $72,000, so Vanguard deducts $72,000. Wrong (B): $96,000 is the total gain, but ordinary income on a disqualifying disposition is limited to the exercise-date bargain element when the sale price exceeds FMV at exercise. Wrong (C): $24,000 incorrectly divides the spread rather than multiplying by 3,000 shares. Wrong (D): ISOs do generate an employer deduction on disqualifying dispositions; only qualifying dispositions produce no deduction.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Section 125 cafeteria plan pre-tax election
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Brookside Medical Group offers a Section 125 cafeteria plan. Employee Javier Morales, earning $95,000, elects to contribute $6,000 for health insurance premiums and $2,850 for a dependent care FSA through the plan. His tax advisor calculates the impact on Morales's taxable compensation. Assuming a combined 30.65% rate (federal + FICA), what is the approximate annual tax savings from making these elections through the cafeteria plan rather than with after-tax dollars?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$950", "$2,713", "$1,839", "$8,850"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 125, amounts elected through a cafeteria plan are excluded from the employee's gross income and are not subject to FICA taxes. Total pre-tax contributions are $6,000 + $2,850 = $8,850. At a combined 30.65% rate, the tax savings are $8,850 x 30.65% = approximately $2,713. These savings arise because the contributions reduce both income tax and FICA. Wrong (A): $950 drastically understates the savings by applying too low a rate. Wrong (C): $1,839 appears to apply the rate to only one of the two benefit elections. Wrong (D): $8,850 is the total contribution amount, not the tax savings.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Comprehensive comp structure evaluation
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Trident Pharmaceuticals hires Chief Medical Officer Dr. Amara Okafor and offers two compensation structures. Option 1: $600,000 salary plus 50,000 NQSOs (exercise price = FMV of $20). Option 2: $400,000 salary plus 30,000 restricted stock shares (FMV $20, 4-year cliff vest) with an 83(b) election. Both assume the stock reaches $80 per share in four years and Dr. Okafor is in the 37% ordinary / 20% LTCG bracket. Ignoring AMT and time value of money, which structure produces lower total federal income tax over the four-year period on the equity component alone?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Option 1, because NQSOs defer taxation until exercise", "Option 2, because the 83(b) election shifts all post-grant appreciation to capital gain rates but the total tax is the same as Option 1", "Option 2, because the 83(b) election converts $1,800,000 of appreciation from ordinary income (37%) to long-term capital gain (20%), saving $306,000 compared to Option 1", "Option 1, because the NQSO spread is partially deductible by the employer, reducing the effective tax cost"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Option 1 NQSO tax: At exercise, spread = ($80 - $20) x 50,000 = $3,000,000 ordinary income, tax = $1,110,000. Option 2 restricted stock with 83(b): At grant, $20 x 30,000 = $600,000 ordinary income, tax = $222,000. At sale ($80), LTCG = ($80 - $20) x 30,000 = $1,800,000, tax = $360,000. Total Option 2 = $582,000. Tax savings = $1,110,000 - $582,000 = $528,000. The $1,800,000 appreciation is taxed at 20% instead of 37%, producing significant savings. Wrong (A): NQSOs defer taxation but the entire spread is ordinary income at 37%, which is more expensive. Wrong (B): The tax is not the same; the rate differential between 37% and 20% on $1,800,000 produces substantial savings. Wrong (D): The employer deduction benefits the employer, not the employee's personal tax liability.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — ESPP qualifying disposition holding periods
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Foster Industries offers an employee stock purchase plan (ESPP) under Section 423. Employee Beth Callahan purchases shares on June 15, 2026. To receive qualifying disposition treatment, Beth must hold the shares until at least which date?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["December 15, 2026, six months after purchase", "June 15, 2027, one year after purchase", "June 15, 2028, two years after purchase", "The later of two years from the offering date or one year from the purchase date"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 423(a), a qualifying disposition of ESPP shares requires the employee to hold the stock for at least two years from the offering date (grant date) and at least one year from the purchase date (exercise date). Both conditions must be met. The later of the two dates determines the earliest qualifying disposition. Wrong (A): Six months is insufficient; the minimum holding period from purchase is one year. Wrong (B): One year from purchase satisfies one condition, but the two-year-from-offering requirement may extend the holding period further. Wrong (C): Two years from purchase may exceed the requirement; the rule measures from the offering date, not the purchase date.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Section 162(m) covered employee definition post-TCJA
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Stratton Industries (publicly traded) paid its CFO, Dana Whitfield, $1,800,000 in total compensation for 2026. Whitfield has served as CFO since 2015 and has never held the CEO or president title. Under current Section 162(m) rules (post-TCJA), the company's tax advisor evaluates the deductibility of Whitfield's compensation. What amount may Stratton deduct, and why?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$1,000,000, because the CFO is a covered employee and the excess is nondeductible", "$1,800,000, because the CFO is not a covered employee under Section 162(m)", "$0, because all compensation to officers of public companies is nondeductible", "$1,800,000 minus any non-performance-based amounts exceeding $1,000,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Post-TCJA, IRC Section 162(m) defines covered employees to include the CEO, CFO, and the three other highest-compensated officers. The TCJA eliminated the CFO exemption that existed under prior law. Whitfield, as CFO, is a covered employee, and Stratton's deduction is limited to $1,000,000. The $800,000 excess is permanently nondeductible. The performance-based compensation exception was also repealed by TCJA. Wrong (B): Under pre-TCJA law the CFO was not a covered employee, but the TCJA added the CFO to the covered employee list effective for tax years after 2017. Wrong (C): The deduction is limited to $1,000,000, not eliminated entirely. Wrong (D): The performance-based compensation exception was repealed by the TCJA; all forms of compensation are subject to the $1,000,000 cap.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — ISO shareholder approval requirement
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Which of the following is a requirement for a stock option plan to grant incentive stock options under Section 422?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The plan must be approved by the board of directors only", "The plan must be approved by the shareholders of the corporation", "The plan must be filed with the SEC before any grants are made", "The plan must limit grants to employees earning over $200,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 422(b)(1), an ISO must be granted under a plan that has been approved by the shareholders of the granting corporation within 12 months before or after the plan is adopted. Shareholder approval is a mandatory statutory requirement. Wrong (A): Board approval alone is insufficient; shareholder approval is also required. Wrong (C): SEC filing is a securities law matter, not a requirement for ISO qualification under the Internal Revenue Code. Wrong (D): There is no income threshold for ISO recipients; the requirement is that they must be employees of the corporation.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — ISOs available only to employees
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Which individuals are eligible to receive incentive stock options under Section 422?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Employees, independent contractors, and directors", "Independent contractors and consultants only", "Employees of the granting corporation or its subsidiaries only", "Any service provider who performs services for the corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 422(a)(1), ISOs may only be granted to individuals who are employees of the corporation granting the option or of a parent or subsidiary corporation. Independent contractors, non-employee directors, and consultants are not eligible for ISOs. Wrong (A): Independent contractors and non-employee directors are excluded from ISO eligibility. Wrong (B): Independent contractors cannot receive ISOs; only employees qualify. Wrong (D): The term is limited to employees as defined under common-law rules, not all service providers.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Stock option repricing and Section 409A
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Horizon Tech granted NQSOs to employee Ryan Costa with an exercise price of $40 per share (equal to FMV at grant). Two years later, the stock price has dropped to $18 per share, and Horizon proposes reducing the exercise price to $18 to retain Costa. A tax advisor evaluates the Section 409A implications of the repricing. What is the primary tax risk?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The repricing triggers immediate taxation of all vested options under Section 83", "The repricing is treated as a new grant with no Section 409A consequences", "The original options lose their NQSO status and become ISOs", "The repricing constitutes a modification that creates a new deferral subject to Section 409A, potentially violating the requirement that the exercise price equal or exceed FMV at the original grant date"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.409A-1(b)(5)(v), reducing the exercise price of an outstanding stock option below the original grant-date FMV is treated as a modification that creates a new stock right. The repriced option may fail to qualify for the stock right exemption from Section 409A (which requires the exercise price to equal or exceed FMV at grant), subjecting the option to 409A's distribution and timing rules. Wrong (A): Repricing does not trigger immediate taxation under Section 83; the issue is Section 409A compliance. Wrong (B): The repricing is not simply a new grant; it is a modification of an existing arrangement with 409A implications. Wrong (C): NQSOs cannot be converted to ISOs through repricing; different statutory requirements apply.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: easy / L2 — NQSO no tax at grant with no readily ascertainable FMV
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Keystone Systems grants employee Paula Chen 5,000 nonqualified stock options on March 1, 2026. The options have an exercise price of $30 per share (equal to FMV). The options are not traded on any market and do not have a readily ascertainable fair market value. What is the tax consequence to Chen at the grant date?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No taxable event occurs at grant", "Chen recognizes ordinary income equal to the Black-Scholes value of the options", "Chen recognizes capital gain on the difference between FMV and exercise price", "Chen must include the exercise price as income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 83 and Treas. Reg. Section 1.83-7, when an NQSO does not have a readily ascertainable FMV at the time of grant, no taxable event occurs at grant. Taxation is deferred until exercise, when the employee recognizes ordinary income on the spread between FMV and the exercise price. Wrong (B): The Black-Scholes model may estimate value, but for tax purposes an option without a readily ascertainable FMV is not taxed at grant. Wrong (C): There is no capital gain recognition at grant because no income event occurs. Wrong (D): The exercise price is not income; it is the amount the employee pays to acquire the stock.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L2 — Section 83 requires transfer of property for taxation
(6, 'Individual Tax Planning: Compensation', $EXPL$Under Section 83, which event triggers the inclusion of property received for services in the service provider's gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The employer's board resolution authorizing the transfer", "The transfer of property that is either transferable by the recipient or no longer subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture", "The date the employee signs the compensation agreement", "The date the employee begins performing services"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 83(a), property transferred in connection with the performance of services is included in the service provider's gross income in the first taxable year in which the property is either transferable or not subject to a substantial risk of forfeiture. Both conditions relate to the recipient's control over and risk of losing the property. Wrong (A): A board resolution authorizing a transfer is not itself a transfer of property and does not trigger Section 83. Wrong (C): Signing a compensation agreement is not a taxable event; actual transfer and lapse of restrictions are required. Wrong (D): The commencement of services is not the triggering event; the transfer of property and lapse of forfeiture risk control the timing.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2)
;

COMMIT;
